<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dazexiang uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">209 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Chen Sheng and Wu Guang uprising</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dazexiang uprising was the first major revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>, begun in 209 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-guang">Wu Guang</a>, leaders of a party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> held up by rain at Dazexiang in present-day Anhui. Facing death for arriving late, they rebelled, and Chen Sheng proclaimed the kingdom of Zhangchu. The rising was crushed within months, but it set off the wider rebellions that overthrew Qin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dazexiang-uprising.jpg" alt="Dazexiang uprising" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_Uprisings.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
